data: {"error":{"message":"Parsing failed. The model generated output that could not be parsed. Please adjust your prompt. See 'failed_generation' for more details.","type":"invalid_request_error","code":"output_parse_failed","failed_generation":"We need to understand the prompt. The user has an ORIGINAL REQUEST: \"get me the headlines, abstract/description, and article links for the top 10 articles from spiegel.de\". AS OF: null. STARTING URL: https://www.spiegel.de. OFFERED RESULT FLOWS: [] (empty). PERMISSION CEILING: {\"allowLinkFollowing\":false,\"linkDepth\":1,\"allowResourceFetching\":false}. CURRENT DECLARATION FEEDBACK: null.\n\nWe need to output JSON envelope with keys \"reasoning\" (optional), \"response\" (optional), \"data\". The data must match the OUTPUT SCHEMA defined earlier. That schema has fields \"requiresNavigation\": boolean, and \"resultFlow\": either an object with id and revision, or null.\n\nWe need to \"Declare the output action for the supplied starting document's contribution to the unchanged instruction. The caller handles other starting documents.\" So we need to decide whether we need navigation beyond this starting document. The request is to \"get me the headlines, abstract/description, and article links for the top 10 articles from spiegel.de\". The starting URL is the root page of spiegel.de. To get the top 10 articles, we likely need to parse the home page to extract the headlines, abstracts, and links. That's possible from the starting document alone. However, we need to consider the permission: allowLinkFollowing = false, linkDepth = 1, allowResourceFetching = false. That means we cannot follow links to fetch additional pages, but we can parse the starting document. However, to extract the top 10 article links, we need to see the list of article links on the home page. The request is to produce the headlines, abstract/description, and article links for the top 10 articles. The starting document probably contains some article listings with links to each article. We may need to extract those from the starting document itself without following those article pages. The request does not require us to fetch the article pages, only to give the links. So we can do that with just the starting document, no navigation required. So requiresNavigation should be false, because we are not required to fetch any additional documents beyond the starting document. The request is not asking to follow links beyond this starting document.\n\nNow resultFlow: The \"OFFERED RESULT FLOWS\" is an empty list, meaning no specific flows offered. The output schema says resultFlow can be either an object with id and revision referencing a offered result flow, or null for selected page data and references only. Since no result flows are offered, we must set resultFlow to null. So we produce data: {\"requiresNavigation\": false, \"resultFlow\": null}.\n\nWe might optionally include a reasoning field in the envelope for clarity. The envelope must be JSON with keys \"reasoning\", \"response\", \"data\". \"response\" optional? It can be omitted or maybe set to null. The specification: \"Your ENTIRE final message must be ONLY the JSON envelope { \"reasoning\"?: string, \"response\"?: string, \"data\": \u003cthe payload\u003e } — raw JSON\". So we can include \"reasoning\" and \"response\" optional. Provide some reasoning text as optional string, maybe \"No navigation needed; resultFlow null as no offered flows.\" This is okay.\n\nThus final output:\n\n{\n  \"reasoning\": \"The request can be satisfied by extracting information from the provided starting document without following any links; navigation beyond this page is not needed. No result flows were offered, so resultFlow is null.\",\n  \"data\": {\n    \"requiresNavigation\": false,\n    \"resultFlow\": null\n  }\n}\n\nMake sure JSON is valid, no extra fields, no code fences.\n\nThus final.","status_code":400}}

